  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 1.07 — Euclidean Invariants\n\nStage 1 has been six notebooks of Euclidean geometry. This one asks what we were actually doing.\n\nA quantity is a **Euclidean invariant** if it is unchanged by every isometry: a function $f$ of a configuration of points with\n\n$$f(g \\cdot p_1, \\ldots, g \\cdot p_k) = f(p_1, \\ldots, p_k) \\qquad \\text{for all } g \\in E(n).$$\n\nThe claim of this notebook is that *all* of Euclidean geometry consists of statements about such functions — and that the invariants can be described completely:\n\n> **Every Euclidean invariant of a finite set of points is a function of the pairwise distances.**\n\nThis is the Euclidean case of what invariant theory calls a **first fundamental theorem** — Weyl's results for the classical groups are the general statement — and it is more useful than it sounds. It says the distance matrix is a complete record: any Euclidean quantity — an area, an angle, a circumradius, the volume of a tetrahedron — must be expressible in distances alone, and we can go looking for the formula knowing one exists. We derive Heron's formula and its higher analogues this way.\n\nThere is one important caveat, and it is the interesting part: the theorem is about $E(n)$, which includes reflections. Drop to the orientation-preserving $SE(n)$ and a new invariant appears that distances cannot see — **chirality**. A left hand and a right hand have identical distance matrices.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. Why distances are enough\n\nThe proof is a two-step reduction, and both steps are things we have already done.\n\n**Step 1 — kill the translation.** Distances only involve differences $p_i - p_j$, so put $q_i = p_i - p_1$ and work with vectors based at the origin. Now only the linear part $A \\in O(n)$ acts.\n\n**Step 2 — the Gram matrix.** Form $G_{ij} = q_i \\cdot q_j$. Under $q \\mapsto Aq$ with $A$ orthogonal,\n\n$$(Aq_i) \\cdot (Aq_j) = q_i^{\\mathsf T}A^{\\mathsf T}A\\,q_j = q_i \\cdot q_j,$$\n\nso $G$ is invariant. Conversely, if two configurations have the same Gram matrix then the map sending one to the other preserves all inner products, and by the argument of 1.02 §1 it extends to an element of $O(n)$. **So the Gram matrix is a complete invariant.**\n\n**Step 3 — Gram from distances.** Polarisation again:\n\n$$G_{ij} = q_i \\cdot q_j = \\tfrac12\\left(|q_i|^2 + |q_j|^2 - |q_i - q_j|^2\\right) = \\tfrac12\\left(d_{1i}^2 + d_{1j}^2 - d_{ij}^2\\right).$$\n\nEvery entry of $G$ is a function of the pairwise distances, and so every invariant is. $\\blacksquare$\n\nThe polarisation identity has now done three separate jobs — proving isometries are linear (1.02), proving Cauchy–Schwarz (1.01), and here proving distances determine everything. It is the single most reused line in Stage 1, and the reason is always the same: **the metric determines the inner product**, so anything the inner product knows, distances know too.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncvec(v) := transpose(matrix(v))$\nvnorm(a) := sqrt(a . a)$\ncross(a, b) := [ a[2]*b[3] - a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1] - a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2] - a[2]*b[1] ]$\n\ndist(p, q) := vnorm(p - q)$\ndistmat(P) := genmatrix( lambda([i,j], ratsimp(dist(P[i], P[j]))), length(P), length(P) )$\n\ngram(P) := block([q],\n  q : makelist(P[i] - P[1], i, 2, length(P)),\n  genmatrix( lambda([i,j], ratsimp(q[i] . q[j])), length(q), length(q) ) )$\n\n/* Gram entries really are built from distances alone */\ngram_from_dist(D) := block([n],\n  n : length(D),\n  genmatrix( lambda([i,j], ratsimp( (D[1][i+1]^2 + D[1][j+1]^2 - D[i+1][j+1]^2)/2 )),\n             n-1, n-1 ) )$\n\n/* A test configuration in the plane */\nPcfg : [ [0,0], [3,0], [1,2], [-1,1] ]$\nDm   : distmat(Pcfg)$\nprint(\"distance matrix = \", Dm)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Gram matrix from coordinates: \", gram(Pcfg))$\nprint(\"Gram matrix from distances  : \", gram_from_dist(Dm))$\nprint(\"difference                  : \", ratsimp(gram(Pcfg) - gram_from_dist(Dm)))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "distance matrix =\n                  matrix([0,3,sqrt(5),sqrt(2)],[3,0,2^(3/2),sqrt(17)],\n                         [sqrt(5),2^(3/2),0,sqrt(5)],\n                         [sqrt(2),sqrt(17),sqrt(5),0])\nGram matrix from coordinates:  matrix([9,3,-3],[3,5,1],[-3,1,2])\nGram matrix from distances  :  matrix([9,3,-3],[3,5,1],[-3,1,2])\ndifference                  :  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])"
     ]
    }
