  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Cashing the theorem: volume from distances alone\n\nThe theorem promises that every Euclidean invariant is a function of distances. Area and volume are Euclidean invariants. So there must be a formula for the area of a triangle in terms of its three side lengths, and for the volume of a tetrahedron in terms of its six edge lengths — even though \"area\" is defined by coordinates and \"distance\" is not.\n\nThe formula is the **Cayley–Menger determinant**. For $n+1$ points in $\\mathbb R^n$ with pairwise distances $d_{ij}$, form the bordered matrix\n\n$$CM = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 1 & \\cdots & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & d_{12}^2 & \\cdots \\\\ 1 & d_{21}^2 & 0 & \\cdots \\\\ \\vdots & & & \\ddots \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad V^2 = \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2^n (n!)^2}\\,\\det CM .$$\n\nFor $n = 2$ this is Heron's formula in disguise; for $n = 3$ it gives the volume of a tetrahedron from its six edges — a formula that is genuinely awkward to derive any other way, and which falls out here for free.\n\nIt also gives a test for **realisability**: a proposed distance matrix comes from actual points in $\\mathbb R^n$ exactly when the appropriate Cayley–Menger determinants have the right signs. Not every symmetric matrix of positive numbers satisfying the triangle inequality is a distance matrix, and this is how you tell.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Cayley–Menger matrix from a distance matrix */\ncayley_menger(D) := block([m, n],\n  n : length(D),\n  m : genmatrix( lambda([i,j],\n        if i = 1 and j = 1 then 0\n        elseif i = 1 or j = 1 then 1\n        else D[i-1][j-1]^2 ), n+1, n+1 ),\n  m )$\n\n/* Squared content of the simplex on n+1 points in R^n */\ncm_volume2(D) := block([n],\n  n : length(D) - 1,\n  ratsimp( (-1)^(n+1) * determinant(cayley_menger(D)) / (2^n * (n!)^2) ) )$\n\n/* --- Triangle: this must reproduce Heron's formula --- */\nDtri : matrix([0, a, b], [a, 0, c], [b, c, 0])$\narea2 : cm_volume2(Dtri)$\nprint(\"Cayley–Menger area² for sides a, b, c:\")$\nprint(\"   \", factor(area2))$\n\n/* Heron: 16·Area² = (a+b+c)(−a+b+c)(a−b+c)(a+b−c) */\nheron2 : (a+b+c)*(-a+b+c)*(a-b+c)*(a+b-c)/16$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Heron's formula   : \", factor(heron2))$\nprint(\"difference        : \", ratsimp(area2 - heron2), \"  — identical\")$\n\n/* --- Tetrahedron: volume from six edge lengths --- */\nPtet : [ [0,0,0], [1,0,0], [0,1,0], [0,0,1] ]$\nDtet : distmat(Ptet)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"regular-ish tetrahedron with vertices at the origin and the three unit points\")$\nprint(\"edge lengths     : \", [Dtet[1][2], Dtet[1][3], Dtet[1][4],\n                              Dtet[2][3], Dtet[2][4], Dtet[3][4]])$\nprint(\"Cayley–Menger V² : \", cm_volume2(Dtet), \"  → V = \", sqrt(cm_volume2(Dtet)))$\nprint(\"direct  |det|/6  : \", abs(determinant(matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])))/6)$\n\n/* --- Realisability: a distance matrix that no points can produce --- */\nDbad : matrix([0, 1, 1, 1], [1, 0, 1, 1], [1, 1, 0, 1], [1, 1, 1, 0])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"four mutually-unit-distant points: V² = \", cm_volume2(Dbad),\n      \"  (positive — a regular tetrahedron of edge 1, so realisable in R³)\")$\nDbad2 : matrix([0, 1, 1], [1, 0, 3], [1, 3, 0])$\nprint(\"triangle with sides 1, 1, 3: area² = \", cm_volume2(Dbad2),\n      \"  (negative — violates the triangle inequality, no such triangle)\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
