  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 3. What distances cannot see: chirality\n\nThe theorem said *Euclidean* invariants, and $E(n)$ contains reflections. If we restrict to the orientation-preserving subgroup\n\n$$SE(n) = \\{x \\mapsto Ax + b : \\det A = +1\\},$$\n\nthen more functions become invariant, and the distance matrix is no longer a complete record.\n\nThe extra invariant is the **sign of the oriented volume**. For $n+1$ points in $\\mathbb R^n$, the determinant $\\det(p_2 - p_1, \\ldots, p_{n+1} - p_1)$ is multiplied by $\\det A$ under an isometry — so it is $SE(n)$-invariant but only $E(n)$-invariant *up to sign*. Its magnitude is a function of distances (that is Cayley–Menger); its sign is not.\n\nConcretely: a configuration and its mirror image have **identical distance matrices** but opposite orientation. They are congruent in $E(n)$ and not in $SE(n)$. Such a pair is called **chiral**, and the phenomenon is not a technicality:\n\n- A left hand cannot be rotated onto a right hand, though every corresponding distance agrees.\n- Two enantiomers of a molecule have identical bond lengths and angles, and can behave completely differently. The cleanest example is carvone: one enantiomer smells of spearmint, the other of caraway, and the only difference is a sign. (Thalidomide is the case usually cited here, but the standard telling is misleading — thalidomide racemises *in vivo*, so administering the single \"safe\" enantiomer would still have produced the harmful one.)\n- In 1.02 the same distinction was $\\det = \\pm 1$ separating rotations from reflections; in 1.01 it was the sign of the triple product.\n\nSo the honest statement of the theorem is: **distances determine a configuration up to $E(n)$**, and the one further bit of information needed to pin it down up to $SE(n)$ is a single sign.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* A deliberately asymmetric tetrahedron and its mirror image in the plane z = 0 */\nPchiral : [ [0,0,0], [2,0,0], [1/2,3,0], [1,1,4] ]$\nPmirror : map(lambda([p], [p[1], p[2], -p[3]]), Pchiral)$\n\nsigned_vol(P) := ratsimp( determinant(matrix(P[2]-P[1], P[3]-P[1], P[4]-P[1]))/6 )$\n\nprint(\"distance matrix, original:\")$\nprint(distmat(Pchiral))$\nprint(\"distance matrix, mirrored:\")$\nprint(distmat(Pmirror))$\nprint(\"identical? \", is( ratsimp(distmat(Pchiral) - distmat(Pmirror)) = zeromatrix(4,4) ))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"signed volume, original: \", signed_vol(Pchiral))$\nprint(\"signed volume, mirrored: \", signed_vol(Pmirror))$\nprint(\"→ same |V|, opposite sign: congruent in E(3), NOT in SE(3)\")$\n\n/* Cayley–Menger, which sees only distances, cannot tell them apart */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Cayley–Menger V² of each: \", cm_volume2(distmat(Pchiral)),\n      \"  and  \", cm_volume2(distmat(Pmirror)))$\nprint(\"as it must be — it is a function of distances, so it is blind to chirality\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
