  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 4. Stage 1 in one table\n\nEverything in Stage 1 was the study of one group and what it fixes.\n\n| Notebook | The question | The invariant |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1.01 | how do we measure? | $a\\cdot b$, $a \\times b$, $[a,b,c]$ — length, area, volume |\n| 1.02 | what preserves distance in the plane? | $E(2)$; four types, classified by fixed points |\n| 1.03 | and in space? | $SO(3)$; axis and angle; $\\exp$ of $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ |\n| 1.04 | which *finite* groups can occur? | $C_n$, $D_n$, $T$, $O$, $I$ — hence five Platonic solids |\n| 1.05 | what if we drop distance? | affine ratios; barycentric coordinates; Ceva, Menelaus |\n| 1.06 | what shapes do quadratics cut out? | eigenvalues and signature of a symmetric matrix |\n| 1.07 | what *is* a Euclidean quantity? | a function of the pairwise distances |\n\nAnd the closing theorem ties it together: a configuration is determined up to $E(n)$ by its distance matrix, and up to $SE(n)$ by that plus one sign. The congruence criteria of school geometry — SSS most obviously — are corollaries.\n\n## The Erlangen ladder\n\nKlein's insight is that this whole analysis can be *repeated with a different group*, and each choice produces a different geometry with a different notion of \"the same\". Stage 1 sat on the bottom rung. The remaining rungs are Stage 2:\n\n| Group | What it fixes | Characteristic invariant | Notebook |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $SE(n)$ | distance + orientation | distances, and chirality | 1.07 |\n| $E(n)$ | distance | pairwise distances | 1.02, 1.07 |\n| Similarities | angle, ratios of distance | shape, not size | — |\n| $\\mathrm{Aff}(n)$ | the line at infinity | ratios along a line, area ratios | 1.05 |\n| $PGL(n{+}1)$ | nothing at all | the **cross-ratio** of four collinear points | 2.03 |\n| Möbius group | circles-and-lines | angles, cross-ratio on $\\hat{\\mathbb C}$ | 2.05 |\n\nRead downwards, structure is progressively surrendered and the invariants get scarcer — but never empty. Even projective geometry, which fixes no distances, no angles, no parallels and no ratios, still has the cross-ratio. Finding out what survives at each level is the subject of Stage 2.\n\nRead upwards, each geometry is the one above it with something extra remembered. Euclidean geometry is affine geometry that has remembered a metric; affine geometry is projective geometry that has remembered which line is at infinity.\n\n## A note on where this ends up\n\nThe Erlangen program describes **homogeneous** spaces — spaces that look the same at every point, because a group acts transitively on them. That is a serious limitation: most spaces are not like that. A lumpy surface has no symmetry group worth the name, and there is no group-theoretic way to say what its geometry is.\n\nRiemann's answer, which Stages 3–4 develop, is to make the structure **local**: put an inner product on each tangent space separately and let it vary from point to point. The invariants then become local too — curvature at a point, rather than a global group of motions. Stage 5 reunites the two pictures by showing that curvature is itself the failure of a local symmetry to close up globally.\n\nBut the constant-curvature spaces of Stage 2 — sphere, plane, hyperbolic plane — are exactly the ones where Klein's picture and Riemann's picture agree, and that is why they come next.\n\n## Next\n\n**Stage 2 opens with 2.01 — The Erlangen Program**, which states Klein's thesis properly and sets up the machinery for changing groups deliberately rather than by accident.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## References\n\n- Blumenthal, L.M. (1953) *Theory and Applications of Distance Geometry*. Oxford: Clarendon Press. The standard source for Cayley–Menger determinants and for realisability of distance matrices.\n- Weyl, H. (1939) *The Classical Groups: Their Invariants and Representations*. Princeton: Princeton University Press. The first and second fundamental theorems of invariant theory, of which the result proved here is the Euclidean case.\n- Klein, F. (1893) 'A comparative review of recent researches in geometry', trans. M.W. Haskell, *Bulletin of the New York Mathematical Society*, 2(10), pp. 215–249. The English translation of the 1872 *Erlangen Programm*; 2.01 discusses it directly.\n- ★ Lee, N.-H. (2020) *Geometry: from Isometries to Special Relativity*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42101-4. Chapter 1 for the congruence results this reframes.\n\nOn chirality: the carvone enantiomers are the standard clean demonstration. The thalidomide story is more complicated than usually told — see Tokunaga, E. *et al.* (2018) 'The thalidomide tragedy: 60 years later', *Chemical Society Reviews*, 47, pp. 5388–5402, on *in vivo* racemisation.\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
